/-!
# Elementary divisors of alternating forms over a principal ideal domain

Let `B` be an alternating bilinear form on a finite free module `M` over a principal ideal domain
`R`. Then `M` has a basis `e₁, …, e_g, f₁, …, f_g, z₁, …, z_r` in which
`B (eᵢ, fᵢ) = dᵢ = -B (fᵢ, eᵢ)` with `dᵢ ≠ 0` and `d₁ ∣ d₂ ∣ ⋯ ∣ d_g`, every other pairing of basis
vectors vanishes, and the `zₖ` lie in the kernel of `B`. In matrix form,
`B = [[0, D, 0], [-D, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0]]` with `D = diag(d₁, …, d_g)`. When `B` is nondegenerate
there are no `zₖ`, so the rank of `M` is even, and the matrix is `[[0, D], [-D, 0]]` with
determinant `(d₁ ⋯ d_g)²`. Over an ordered ring such as `ℤ` the `dᵢ` can be taken positive, and
since an integral change of basis has determinant `±1`, the determinant of an integral form in
*every* basis is `(d₁ ⋯ d_g)²`.

This is the normal form behind the type of a polarization: an integral Riemann form on the period
lattice of a complex torus is a nondegenerate alternating form on a free `ℤ`-module of rank `2g`,
and its elementary divisors `(d₁, …, d_g)` are the type. Over a field every `dᵢ` is a unit, which
is the case of the symplectic basis theorem.

The proof is Frobenius's. Since `R` is Noetherian, some value `d = B e f` generates an ideal
maximal among those generated by single values of `B`. A Bézout combination shows that `d` divides
every `B e u` and every `B f u`. If `B f u = d * b`, the vector `e + u + b • e` pairs with `f` to
`d` again, so `d` also divides every `B u v`
(`LinearMap.BilinForm.IsAlt.exists_forall_dvd_apply`). Divisibility makes the projection
`u ↦ u + b • e - a • f`, where `B e u = d * a` and `B f u = d * b`, integral, so the span of `e, f`
splits off orthogonally (`LinearMap.BilinForm.IsAlt.isCompl_span_pair_orthogonal_of_dvd`). The
orthogonal complement is again finite free, of rank two less, and every value of `B` on it is a
multiple of `d`, which gives the divisibility of the next elementary divisor; induction on the rank
finishes the proof.

## Main results

* `LinearMap.BilinForm.IsAlt.exists_forall_dvd_apply`: over a principal ideal ring an alternating
  form has a value `B e f` dividing all of its values.
* `LinearMap.BilinForm.IsAlt.exists_basis_toMatrix_eq_fromBlocks`: **the elementary divisor normal
  form** `[[0, D, 0], [-D, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0]]` of an alternating form on a finite free module over a
  principal ideal domain.
* `LinearMap.BilinForm.IsAlt.exists_basis_toMatrix_eq_fromBlocks_of_nondegenerate`: the normal form
  `[[0, D], [-D, 0]]` of a nondegenerate alternating form, and
  `LinearMap.BilinForm.IsAlt.even_finrank_of_isPrincipalIdealRing`: the module then has even rank.
* `LinearMap.BilinForm.IsAlt.exists_basis_toMatrix_eq_fromBlocks_pos_of_nondegenerate`: over an
  ordered principal ideal domain, such as `ℤ`, the elementary divisors can be taken positive.
* `LinearMap.BilinForm.det_toMatrix_eq_prod_sq_of_toMatrix_eq_fromBlocks`: over `ℤ` the
  determinant of the form in every basis is the square of the product of its elementary divisors.
* `LinearMap.BilinForm.eq_of_toMatrix_eq_fromBlocks`: over `ℤ` **the elementary divisors are
  unique**: two nonnegative divisibility chains `d`, `d'` for which `B` has matrices
  `[[0, D], [-D, 0]]` and `[[0, D'], [-D', 0]]` in two bases are equal.
* `LinearMap.BilinForm.IsAlt.polarizationType`: **the type** `(d₁, …, d_g)` of a nondegenerate
  alternating form on a finite free `ℤ`-module of rank `2g`, with `IsAlt.polarizationType_eq`
  (any nonnegative divisibility chain giving the normal form is the type) and
  `IsAlt.det_toMatrix_eq_prod_polarizationType_sq` (`det B = (d₁ ⋯ d_g)²`).

The uniqueness over `ℤ` reduces to that of the Smith normal form
(`Matrix.smith_normal_form_unique`): `[[0, D], [-D, 0]]` is `-J` times `diag(D, D)`, where `J`
is the standard symplectic matrix `Matrix.J`, a change of basis makes `diag(D, D)` and
`diag(D', D')` equivalent under `GL(ℤ)`, and interleaving the two copies
(`TauCeti.finSumFinInterleaveEquiv`), `diag(d₁, d₁, …, d_g, d_g)`, gives divisibility chains.

## References

* G. Frobenius, *Theorie der linearen Formen mit ganzen Coefficienten*, J. Reine Angew. Math. 86
  (1879), 146–208.
* C. Birkenhake and H. Lange, *Complex Abelian Varieties*, 2nd ed., Grundlehren 302 (2004),
  Chapter 3 (the type of a polarization).
-/

